# === Basics ==================================================================
# Public URL of the app (locally http://localhost:3000, your domain in prod).
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Environment: development | staging | production. Each environment has its OWN
# Digistore24 product set — `node run.mjs ds24-sync --env dev|staging|prod`
# maintains one at a time (see docs/environments.md; staging is optional).
# Receive IPNs locally: node run.mjs ds24-tunnel (Cloudflare).
APP_ENV=development
#
# The deployed domains, for a LOCALLY-run `ds24-sync --env prod|staging`.
# APP_URL itself stays local — a non-local value switches off the development
# login (lib/auth/dev-login.ts) and locks you out of your own app:
# APP_URL_PROD=
# APP_URL_STAGING=

# === Database (Postgres) =====================================================
# Locally via docker-compose (see docker-compose.yml). In prod: managed Postgres
# (Railway, Render, Fly.io and DigitalOcean all offer one next to the app —
# docs/DEPLOY.md, or ask Claude Code for the skill setup-hosting).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://app:app@localhost:15432/app
# How the LOCAL database is started. You do not have to set this: on the first
# start your machine is looked at, and the answer is written in here.
#   docker  Postgres 16 in a container (docker-compose.yml) — chosen whenever
#           there is a Docker that answers. It is the same Postgres your app
#           runs against on the server later.
#   local   Postgres 16 from an npm package, no Docker needed (about 60 MB, is
#           fetched on first use). Chosen on every machine without Docker.
# The second one is the second-best way, not a worse database: it is the real
# PostgreSQL 16, so migrations, scripts and DATABASE_URL are unaffected. What
# differs is that your local setup then deviates a little from production.
# Once the line is here it stays put, and that is deliberate — a Docker Desktop
# that did not start this morning looks exactly like a machine that never had
# one, and re-deciding would point your project at a second, empty database.
# Change it by hand only while the database holds nothing you want to keep.
# Anything other than these two values is refused (scripts/db/driver.mjs).
# DB_DRIVER=
# Port on which the local Docker Postgres is reachable. 15432 instead of the
# usual 5432, so that an already installed Postgres or another project is not
# in the way. If that one is taken as well, `node run.mjs start` finds the next free
# port itself and enters it here and in DATABASE_URL.
# DB_PORT=15432
# Name of the Docker Compose project the local database belongs to — its
# container and, more importantly, its data volume are named after it. You do
# not have to set this either: the first start derives one from this folder's
# PATH and writes it in here.
# Compose would otherwise name the project after the FOLDER, and a folder called
# `test`, `app` or `demo` is one somebody has had before — the new app then
# adopts the old app's volume, finds a schema it never wrote and dies on its
# first migration with "type … already exists". The path decides instead, so two
# same-named folders are two databases.
# Once the line is here it stays put: it is what lets you rename or move the
# project folder without the app losing its database. Change it by hand only
# while the database holds nothing you want to keep.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=
# Size of the DB connection pool per process (default 10). On a single
# server 10–20 is fine; on serverless keep it low + use a connection pooler.
# DB_POOL_MAX=10
# The two accounts `node run.mjs db-seed` creates for development (defaults
# owner@example.com and customer@example.com). Your own addresses belong here,
# not in front of the command — `VAR=… node run.mjs db-seed` is shell syntax
# that PowerShell does not have. See docs/database.md → Seed data.
# SEED_OWNER_EMAIL=
# SEED_MEMBER_EMAIL=

# === Auth.js =================================================================
# Locally this is filled in by `node run.mjs start`. For STAGING/PROD generate
# one in the host's secret management:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
AUTH_SECRET=
# Needed behind a proxy/PaaS so that the dynamic host is accepted.
AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true

# === Cron ====================================================================
# Bearer token that guards POST /api/cron — the way IN to the scheduled jobs
# from outside the app (docs/cron.md). Without it that endpoint refuses to run
# at all, so it can never be left open as a "delete my data" URL.
#
# You do NOT need this for the jobs to run. The app schedules them itself while
# it is up; this is only for a host that would rather do the timing, and for
# `node run.mjs cron`, which generates one here on first use.
# Random secret:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# CRON_SECRET=

# Reference copies of what is set on the deployed hosts, so you can ask them
# whether their jobs are running without a shell there:
#   node run.mjs cron --list --url https://your-app.example.com
# The suffixed names are the convention SETUP_KEY_PROD, DIAGNOSTICS_SECRET_PROD
# and MEDIA_S3_*_PROD already use — the plain key above always means THIS
# machine's environment. The command picks the one whose APP_URL_* hostname
# matches the address you gave it, and refuses rather than guessing when none
# does: a secret that opens "delete my data" jobs is never sent to a host it was
# not provisioned for. Neither value is ever generated here — set it in the
# host's secret storage (docs/DEPLOY.md) and copy the same value in.
# CRON_SECRET_PROD=
# CRON_SECRET_STAGING=

# Bearer token that lets you read a DEPLOYED app's own error window:
#   node run.mjs errors --url https://your-app.example.com
# A deployed app has no .dev/dev.log, so it keeps a bounded, redacted window of
# its own stderr instead and answers with what the SAME parser finds in it
# (docs/DEPLOY.md → Proving it works). Without this variable the endpoint
# answers 404 with an empty body — indistinguishable from a route that was
# never built, which is the shipped state.
# Set the SAME value in the host's secret storage and here; nothing git-tracked
# ever holds it.
# The same token opens the ONE diagnostics door that spends money:
#   node run.mjs ai-check --live
# asks the app for one real model call per binding, to find out whether the AI
# keys actually work rather than merely being present (docs/ai-providers.md).
# Random secret:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# DIAGNOSTICS_SECRET=

# Switches the collector off entirely — no wrapper around process.stderr.write
# at all. The window is then always empty and `errors --url` says so.
# DIAGNOSTICS_CAPTURE=off

# Optional override for the product name in the mails (subject, heading,
# footer). Without it the mails use NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME below — set that one
# and the mails are covered too.
# APP_NAME=My App

# Name of the app in the interface (sidebar, browser tab, home page).
# Without this value the default from lib/app.ts applies. NEXT_PUBLIC_ means:
# the value is in the browser too — so only the name here, never a secret.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=My App

# --- Development login (DEV only) ---
# As long as NO mail delivery is configured and APP_ENV=development applies (and
# APP_URL points at localhost), you can sign in without a magic link and
# without a password — so that you can try the app out right away.
# As soon as Postmark or SMTP is set up, it disappears automatically.
# In STAGING/PROD it never exists; there mail delivery is mandatory and the app
# does not even start without it. To set it up: node run.mjs mail-setup
# Hard off switch:
# DEV_LOGIN=off

# --- Sign-in (default): email token / magic link ---
# Configure exactly ONE mail transport — Postmark OR SMTP.
#
# The sender MUST be an address on the app's own domain (APP_URL) — a sign-in
# mail whose links point at your domain but whose From lives on another one is
# the exact shape of a phishing mail, and enough recipient reports put your
# domain on Google's Safe Browsing "Dangerous site" list. STAGING/PROD refuse
# to start otherwise (docs/auth-setup.md). Subdomains count as the same domain.
# A foreign sender as a deliberate, informed decision must name its domain:
# EMAIL_FROM_FOREIGN_DOMAIN=their-domain.com   # the Safe Browsing risk is yours
#
# Sender address (fallback if no transport-specific one is set below).
EMAIL_FROM=login@your-domain.de
#
# Option A: Postmark (server token + verified sender/sender signature)
# POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN=
# POSTMARK_SENDER=login@your-domain.de
# POSTMARK_MESSAGE_STREAM=outbound
#
# Option B: SMTP (any mail server / mailbox)
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.yourprovider.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false           # true = Port 465 (SSL), false = 587 (STARTTLS)
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM=login@your-domain.de

# --- Sign-in (optional): Google OAuth ---
# Setup + approval take time (Google reviews the app). Guide: docs/auth-setup.md
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Smoke sign-in for the DEPLOYED app ---
# `node run.mjs smoke --url https://…` needs a session to render the protected
# pages, and the development login does not exist outside DEV. These are the
# credentials of a dedicated MEMBER account (deliberately never an owner) that
# `node run.mjs smoke-account` provisions on the deployed app and writes in
# here itself — a random password, scoped to the host in APP_URL_PROD /
# APP_URL_STAGING and never sent anywhere else. Local only, gitignored, never
# a host secret: the deployed app stores the hash and nothing more.
# Re-running smoke-account rotates the password.
# SMOKE_PROD_EMAIL=
# SMOKE_PROD_PASSWORD=
# SMOKE_STAGING_EMAIL=
# SMOKE_STAGING_PASSWORD=

# === Digistore24 =============================================================
# What is the SAME for every installation is not here but in
# lib/digistore/config.mjs: the API address, the developer key of the template,
# the permissions it asks for and the public redirect page. Only what belongs to
# YOUR installation lives here.
#
# The API key and IPN passphrase belong in here — the app reads them from the
# environment (lib/digistore/settings.ts). There is deliberately NO interface to
# enter them: the key belongs to the installation, not to a user.
#
# The connection wizard writes both values here itself:
#
#   node run.mjs ds24-connect
#
# It opens the browser, you grant access at Digistore24, and the script fetches
# the key. None of this has to be entered by hand:
# DIGISTORE_API_KEY=
#
# The IPN passphrase (SHA512 check of incoming events) and a stable domain_id
# for the IPN hookup are written here by `node run.mjs ds24-sync` itself —
# do not set them by hand. The unsuffixed keys are what the app READS, and they
# always mean the environment this machine runs as (APP_ENV):
# DIGISTORE_IPN_PASSPHRASE=
# DIGISTORE_IPN_DOMAIN_ID=
#
# A locally-run `ds24-sync --env prod|staging` stores that environment's values
# under suffixed keys instead — reference copies, written by the sync. Copy them
# into the host's secret store as the UNSUFFIXED keys and redeploy; until then
# the deployed app rejects every IPN signature:
# DIGISTORE_IPN_PASSPHRASE_PROD=
# DIGISTORE_IPN_DOMAIN_ID_PROD=
# DIGISTORE_IPN_PASSPHRASE_STAGING=
# DIGISTORE_IPN_DOMAIN_ID_STAGING=
#
# Products are managed centrally in config/digistore-products.json; the
# productIds (per environment) and the app's product group id are written in
# there by scripts/ds24/sync-products.mjs (not here).
#
# For the product approval (go-live): which marketplace it is requested from.
# That normally follows the PRODUCT's own "language" in
# config/digistore-products.json (German → Germany/1, otherwise USA/2). These
# two are only the fallback and the override:
#   APP_LANG                 used for products that name no language of their own
#   DIGISTORE_SITEOWNER_ID   forces ONE marketplace for every product.
#                            Only 1 (Germany), 2 (USA), 3 (UK) and 4 (Ireland)
#                            are resellers and have a product approval at all.
#                            Any other value is a Direct Seller — then there is
#                            no approval step, and the reminder switches itself
#                            off instead of nagging about something that does
#                            not exist.
# APP_LANG=de
# DIGISTORE_SITEOWNER_ID=
#
# The session greeting checks once a day (one listProducts call) whether the
# synced products are approved for sale yet, and says one line if not
# (scripts/ds24/_approval.mjs). Switch that check off with:
# DIGISTORE_APPROVAL_CHECK=off
#
# Test purchases (DEV only): checkout links append the Digistore24 test-payment
# parameter by themselves, so you can buy through the real checkout without
# setting the cookie by hand. Only in DEV on localhost — never in STAGING/PROD
# (lib/digistore/testpay.ts; inspect/rotate: node run.mjs ds24-testpay).
# Hard off switch:
# DS24_TESTPAY=off
#
# Looking at the buy forms BEFORE the products exist (DEV only): open
# http://localhost:3000/plans?preview=checkout — every card then shows the buy
# form instead of "not created at Digistore24 yet". It asks Digistore24 nothing,
# produces no link and buys nothing; pressing Buy fails exactly as it otherwise
# would. Use it instead of putting dummy ids into config/digistore-products.json
# — that file is tracked by git and the leftover gets committed.
# Only in DEV on localhost (lib/digistore/preview.ts; docs/digistore-integration.md).
# Hard off switch:
# DS24_PLANS_PREVIEW=off

# === AI providers ============================================================
# The five AI companies this app can talk to. You need AT MOST ONE of them, and
# which one is a decision you make per task rather than per app — see
# `lib/ai/providers/` and `config/ai-models.json`.
#
# UNCOMMENT ANY ONE OF THE FIVE — that is the whole setup. The app ships bound
# to "auto", which means "run on whichever key is here", so the one you pick is
# the one it uses, on that company's current default model. You do not have to
# choose a company up front, and you are not stuck with the one you pick.
#
# There is nothing else to switch on: a provider with a key is available, one
# without is not, and a task bound BY NAME to a provider with no key is refused
# by name rather than failing at a customer's first request. What runs where,
# and what one call costs: node run.mjs ai-check
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# GEMINI_API_KEY=...
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=...
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
#
# The in-app assistant runs on one of these keys too — on whichever provider her
# `chat` task resolves to (config/ai-models.json → tasks.chat, "auto" as
# shipped). WITHOUT any key the chat page stays reachable but shows a notice
# instead of a chat; it never half-works and never fails silently.
#
# Whether the app has an assistant AT ALL is not decided here: that is
# `config/ai-chat.json` ("enabled"), because it is a property of the product and
# not of the machine. Both have to hold — see lib/ai/chat-config.ts. She is
# billed per message; what one answer costs is in docs/ai-chat.md.

# --- Media: where uploaded and generated files live -------------------------
#
# Pictures, video, recordings and the files you sell. In DEVELOPMENT nothing has
# to be set: files go to .data/media/ on this machine and everything works.
#
# ONLINE THAT IS NOT AN OPTION, AND THE APP WILL NOT START WITHOUT A BUCKET.
# A local disk is a development convenience, not storage. The next redeploy
# loses every file; a second instance cannot see what the first one wrote, so a
# customer's picture is there roughly half the time and nobody can reproduce it
# on the machine they test on. Because that failure only appears AFTER the app
# is successful, lib/env-guard.ts refuses to start in STAGING and PROD rather
# than warning about it.
#
# SEVEN providers are carried, and one big one is NOT. The app signs its own
# requests (no SDK), so "S3-compatible" is only true of the features this driver
# actually uses — presigned GET and PUT with AWS4-HMAC-SHA256, a server-side
# copy, and `response-content-*` overrides on a signed GET:
#
#   Amazon S3 · DigitalOcean Spaces · Cloudflare R2 · Backblaze B2 ·
#   Hetzner Object Storage · MinIO · Wasabi
#
# NOT CARRIED: Google Cloud Storage. It is the provider you are most likely to
# already have, so this is stated rather than left to a 403. Three mismatches,
# all in the signing and none of them a setting:
#
#   1. Google documents `GOOG4-HMAC-SHA256` as the algorithm for a V4 signature;
#      this app writes `AWS4-HMAC-SHA256` (lib/media/sigv4.mjs).
#   2. The server-side copy sends `x-amz-copy-source` and
#      `x-amz-metadata-directive: REPLACE` (lib/media/s3.ts); Google's own
#      migration reference says to change `x-amz-*` headers to `x-goog-*`.
#   3. A signed GET carries `response-content-type` and
#      `response-content-disposition`, which that reference does not document.
#
# The first two are the course-video upload and the copy that makes it a promise
# rather than a snapshot, so this is not a corner. It is a SECOND SIGNER, not a
# config flag — which is why the answer here is a sentence and not a `goog`
# driver: an unmeasured second signer is code claiming support nobody verified,
# and it would fail in the field rather than in a check.
#
# What would overturn it: a GCS bucket with HMAC keys and a green
# `node run.mjs media-check`. That command writes, reads, HEADs, copies and
# deletes a real object — so it exercises all three mismatches. If it comes back
# green, the exclusion was wrong and this text should say so.
#
# The `setup-hosting` skill books a carried one alongside the database.
#
# MEDIA_DRIVER=s3
# MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT=https://fra1.digitaloceanspaces.com
# MEDIA_S3_REGION=fra1
# MEDIA_S3_BUCKET=my-app-media
# MEDIA_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...
# MEDIA_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
#
# 🚨 MEDIA_S3_REGION is only optional on SOME providers. It defaults to `auto`,
# which is what Cloudflare R2 documents and what MinIO ignores — and AWS S3,
# Wasabi and Backblaze B2 VALIDATE the string and answer 403 without it. Unset
# against one of those, the first upload fails after the customer has already
# waited for their file to travel, with `SignatureDoesNotMatch` as the only clue
# and the credentials as the obvious suspect. `node run.mjs media-check` says so
# in words; docs/DEPLOY.md lists it as required for everything except R2.
#
# Where a BROWSER reads public files — a CDN or a custom domain in front of the
# same bucket. Optional: without it public files are served through a signed
# address like everything else, which is correct and simply less cacheable.
# Set it and product images reach visitors without touching your app at all.
# MEDIA_S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://media.example.com
#
# What may be uploaded, how big, and how long a private address stays valid is
# NOT here — that is config/media.json, because it is a property of the product
# rather than of the machine.
#
# DEV only: where the local driver puts files. Gitignored.
# MEDIA_LOCAL_DIR=.data/media
#
# Reference copies for ANOTHER environment's store — same idea as
# DIGISTORE_IPN_PASSPHRASE_PROD above: the plain MEDIA_S3_* keys always mean
# "the environment this machine runs as" and are never edited to point
# elsewhere. A locally-run `content-media-sync --env prod`, `content-apply
# --env prod` or `kb-media-sync --env prod` reads these instead — that is how
# the production store gets this app's content at go-live (docs/content.md).
# MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT_PROD=
# MEDIA_S3_REGION_PROD=
# MEDIA_S3_BUCKET_PROD=
# MEDIA_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID_PROD=
# MEDIA_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_PROD=
# MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT_STAGING=
# MEDIA_S3_REGION_STAGING=
# MEDIA_S3_BUCKET_STAGING=
# MEDIA_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID_STAGING=
# MEDIA_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_STAGING=

# Time zone used to RENDER dates and times to users. Pinned so a page is
# server-rendered and hydrated in the same zone — otherwise the first component
# showing a clock time mismatches on hydration. Data is stored in UTC either
# way; this only affects display.
APP_TIME_ZONE=Europe/Berlin

# Once a day the session greeting asks the public template repo on GitHub
# (digistore24/ds24-appkit) whether its GUIDANCE (CLAUDE.md, docs/,
# .claude/skills/) has been improved since this app was copied — one GET of one
# public file, no query string, no body, nothing about you or your app, and no
# server of ours involved. `off` switches it off entirely;
# `node run.mjs update` keeps working by hand. Details: docs/updates.md
# TEMPLATE_UPDATE_CHECK=off

# === Setting an environment up from your agent (docs/setup-mcp.md) ===
# The key your coding agent uses to reach THIS app's setup surface. Minted on
# /dashboard/admin/setup-keys, in the environment it acts on, and shown exactly
# once — a lost key is replaced, not recovered.
#
# 🚨 It belongs HERE and in no file git tracks. The MCP wiring that
# `node run.mjs setup-init` writes carries the command and nothing else: an
# `env` block inside an MCP server declaration is the ordinary way to configure
# one, and doing it that way would commit a production credential on the first
# `git add -A`.
#
# The suffixed names are the convention DIGISTORE_IPN_PASSPHRASE_PROD and
# MEDIA_S3_*_PROD already use — the plain key always means THIS machine's
# environment, the suffixed ones are reference copies for the others. The
# destination comes from APP_URL / APP_URL_STAGING / APP_URL_PROD above and is
# never something a tool call can name.
# SETUP_KEY=
# SETUP_KEY_STAGING=
# SETUP_KEY_PROD=

# The same convention for the diagnostics bearer above: the plain name is THIS
# machine's environment, the suffixed ones are the reference copies of what is
# set on the staging and production hosts. `node run.mjs errors --url …` picks
# the one whose APP_URL_* hostname matches the address you gave it, and refuses
# rather than guessing when none does — a secret is never sent to a host it was
# not configured for.
# DIAGNOSTICS_SECRET_STAGING=
# DIAGNOSTICS_SECRET_PROD=
